registerChapter({
  id: 'variant',
  num: '37',
  phase: 'Phase 8 · Control and termination',
  title: 'A loop that terminates, and owns a cell',
  blurb: 'The variant rule — an invariant indexed by the number of turns left — proved by induction on that index, and then spent on a loop that rewrites a heap cell every turn and stops.',

  orient: {
    youWill: [
      'Say why the number that buys termination goes into the <i>invariant</i> rather than into the program, and what a variant written as an expression would cost instead.',
      'Prove <code>hoare_while_variant</code> by induction on the index, and point at the three lines where the loop&rsquo;s derivation is <i>built</i> rather than taken apart.',
      'Refute the version of the rule whose postcondition is <code>I 0</code>, and name the case that fails — a loop may stop before the index reaches zero.',
      'Verify a loop whose termination is a theorem, and open a <code>BExpr</code> guard with a <code>show</code> that unfolds the boolean decision at the same time.',
      'Invent an invariant that carries a store fact <i>and</i> an ownership claim, and prove the two obligations it generates.',
      'Say what happens to the step obligation when the body jumps the counter to zero instead of decrementing it, and repair it by re-indexing.'
    ],
    needs: [
      'Unit 36: the invariant rule, and what the exit conjunct is for.',
      'Unit 35: <code>bTrue</code>, <code>bFalse</code>, and the difference between the two readings of a triple.',
      'Unit 22: <code>Hoare</code> as an existential over final states, and named arguments <code>(Q := …)</code>.',
      'Unit 20: <code>induction n with | zero | succ n ih</code>.',
      'Unit 18: <code>BExpr.eval</code>, <code>decide</code>, and truncated <code>Nat</code> subtraction.'
    ],
    payoff: 'This is the last theorem the course proves and the last program it verifies. What comes after it is not more machinery: it is two questions about the machinery you have — which of it was ever about heaps, and what this language cannot say.'
  },

  blocks: [

    /* ============================================================ brief ==== */

    {t:'p', h:"One natural number, and it does not go in the program. Nothing in <code>Cmd</code> changes on this page; <code>countdown</code> is the same loop Unit 36 specified. What changes is the invariant: instead of one assertion that survives every turn, a <i>family</i> of them, indexed by how many turns are left. The index is the number that buys termination, and putting it in the invariant rather than in the syntax is the whole design."},

    {t:'p', h:"So: one theorem, then two programs. The theorem is <code>hoare_while_variant</code>, the loop rule in the total reading. Then <code>countdown_spec</code>, worked in full — the first triple in this course whose program has no fixed number of steps, so the final state cannot be named and the derivation has to be built. Then the capstone: a loop that reads a counter, rewrites a heap cell on every turn, and stops, verified with an invariant that makes a claim about the store and an ownership claim about memory at the same time. Nothing before this page has written one of those."},

    /* ==================================================== shape of the rule ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Where the number goes'},

    {t:'p', h:"The natural place to put it is the program's own arithmetic. A loop terminates if some quantity strictly decreases and cannot decrease for ever; in <code>countdown</code> that quantity is the counter, and the counter is an <code>Atom</code> the language can evaluate. So: add a variant expression <code>e</code> to the rule, and ask that one turn of the body strictly lower <code>e.eval σ</code>."},

    {t:'p', h:"Two things break under that. A body that lowers <code>e</code> by an unknown amount hands you a descending sequence of naturals, and turning <i>this drops every turn</i> into <i>this stops</i> is well-founded induction — in the concrete case of <code>Nat</code>, strong induction, in which the hypothesis is available at every smaller number rather than at the predecessor. The only induction on <code>Nat</code> this course has is <code>induction n with | zero | succ n ih</code>, which gives you the predecessor and nothing else. And every step of the argument would run through <code>Atom.eval</code> and the store, so the arithmetic would be tangled with the assertions instead of sitting beside them."},

    {t:'p', h:"Turn it round. Let the index <i>be</i> the number of turns left, and let the invariant depend on it. Then the body is asked for something the predecessor form can state: entered at index <code>n + 1</code>, it leaves you at index <code>n</code>. Ordinary induction on the index is then enough, and no arithmetic on expressions appears anywhere. The price is that the index has to fall by exactly one per turn, which is a real restriction and is measured at the end of this page."},

    {t:'txt', cap:'The rule on paper. <code>I</code> is a family of assertions indexed by a natural number; the two premises are the step and the stop; the conclusion is a <b>total</b> triple, for every starting index.',
     src:"        for every n:   { I (n+1) ∧ b }  c  { I n }        ← the body drops the index by one\n                                I 0  ⟹  ¬b                    ← at index zero the guard is off\n        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n            for every n:   { I n }  while b do c  { ∃ m. I m ∧ ¬b }"},

    {t:'anat', tag:'verified',
     src:"theorem hoare_while_variant {I : Nat → Assertion} {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd}\n    (hstep : ∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (I (n + 1)) (bTrue b)) c (I n))\n    (hstop : ∀ σ h, I 0 σ h → b.eval σ = false) :\n    ∀ n, Hoare (I n) (.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) := by",
     parts:[
       {m:'{I : Nat → Assertion}', h:"The whole difference from Unit 36. An assertion for each index, and the caller chooses the family — which is a harder thing to invent than a single invariant, because you must decide not only what stays true but what the count is counting."},
       {m:'Hoare (aAnd (I (n + 1)) (bTrue b)) c (I n)', h:"The step obligation, and it is <b>total</b>: given the invariant at <code>n + 1</code> and the guard, the body has a run and that run lands at index <code>n</code>. A partial premise would not do."},
       {m:'∀ σ h, I 0 σ h → b.eval σ = false', h:"The stop obligation. At index zero the guard is off, so there is nothing left to run. This is the only place the family is pinned to anything: without it, <code>I</code> could be any family at all and the loop could run for ever."},
       {m:'aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)', h:"The postcondition. You end at <i>some</i> index with the guard false — not necessarily at zero. Which index is not knowable in advance, and writing <code>I 0</code> there makes the theorem false."}
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"The stop obligation is an entailment wearing different clothes. <code>I 0 ⊢ bFalse b</code> unfolds to <code>∀ σ h, I 0 σ h → b.eval σ = false</code>, which is what the binder list says, so either spelling typechecks against the rule."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The same theorem with the stop obligation written as an entailment. The proof is the rule itself, applied — the two statements are the same type after unfolding <code>Entails</code> and <code>bFalse</code>.',
     src:"example {I : Nat → Assertion} {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd}\n    (hstep : ∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (I (n + 1)) (bTrue b)) c (I n))\n    (hstop : I 0 ⊢ bFalse b) :\n    ∀ n, Hoare (I n) (.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) :=\n  hoare_while_variant hstep hstop"},

    {t:'p', h:"Now the reason the step obligation is a total triple. Unit 36's rule inverts a run it is handed; this one has no run to invert, because the conclusion is total and a total triple obliges you to <i>produce</i> one. The run of the loop is built storey by storey out of runs of the body, so the body's run has to come from somewhere, and a partial premise never produces one — it consumes one. Feed the precondition to a partial premise and Lean does not even get as far as complaining about the conclusion:"},

    {t:'state', cap:"Compiling <code>obtain ⟨s₁, hex₁, hI₁⟩ := hstep n σ h ⟨hI, hbv⟩</code> with <code>hstep</code> declared <code>PartialHoare</code> instead of <code>Hoare</code>. The line-and-column prefix is dropped here and everywhere below.",
     src:"error: Application type mismatch: The argument\n  hI\nhas type\n  I (n + 1) σ h\nof sort `Prop` but is expected to have type\n  Store\nof sort `Type` in the application\n  State.mk hI"},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>PartialHoare</code>'s third binder is the final state, so the bracket meant as a precondition is read as a <code>State</code> and the mismatch lands on the first component. The honest reading of that error is the design point: a partial premise wants the body's run handed to it, and here there is nothing to hand it."},

    {t:'p', h:"One degenerate case says what the index is <i>for</i>, and it is short enough to have in mind during the proof. Take a constant family — <code>I n</code> the same assertion for every <code>n</code>. Then the stop obligation says the guard is false wherever that assertion holds, so the loop never runs a single turn, and the rule collapses to the one-line theorem below. Everything the variant rule says beyond that comes from the index changing."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'A constant family makes the conclusion a triple about a loop with no turns in it. One <code>Exec.loopFalse</code>, no induction.',
     src:"theorem constant_variant_is_no_loop {I : Assertion} {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd}\n    (hstop : ∀ σ h, I σ h → b.eval σ = false) :\n    Hoare I (.loop b c) (aAnd I (bFalse b)) :=\n  fun σ h hI => ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.loopFalse (hstop σ h hI), hI, hstop σ h hI⟩"},

    /* ============================================== the early exit ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The postcondition, and the loop that leaves early'},

    {t:'p', h:"The postcondition asks for <i>some</i> index with the guard false. The tidier statement would fix it: you started at <code>n</code>, the body drops the index by one each turn, so you finish at zero. Write <code>aAnd (I 0) (bFalse b)</code> and the proof runs into a wall at exactly one place."},

    {t:'state', cap:'The <code>succ</code> branch of the induction, with the guard false, in the version whose postcondition is <code>I 0</code>. Five context lines shared with every other branch (<code>I</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>hstep</code>, <code>hstop</code>) are dropped; nothing else is.',
     src:"case succ.false\nn : Nat\nih : Hoare (I n) (Cmd.loop b c) (aAnd (I 0) (bFalse b))\nσ : Store\nh : Heap\nhI : I (n + 1) σ h\nhbv : BExpr.eval σ b = false\n⊢ I 0 { store := σ, heap := h }.store { store := σ, heap := h }.heap"},

    {t:'p', h:"The index says <code>n + 1</code> turns remain and the guard says the loop stops now. Nothing in the rule forbids that: the stop obligation constrains index zero and says nothing about the others, so a guard is free to go false early. The state you land in satisfies <code>I (n + 1)</code>, and the goal wants <code>I 0</code>, and the two are different assertions."},

    {t:'p', h:"That is a stuck proof, which is a fact about the proof. The statement itself is worse than stuck. Take a family whose index-zero member is <code>aFalse</code> and whose every other member is <code>aTrue</code>, with a guard that is false at every store. Both premises hold — the stop obligation because nothing satisfies <code>aFalse</code>, the step obligation because nothing satisfies <code>bTrue</code> of a guard that never fires — and the conclusion at index 1 would say that running the loop lands in <code>aFalse</code>."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The <code>I 0</code> postcondition, refuted. The last declaration is the same family under the real rule, which goes through: the difference is the existential, and nothing else.',
     src:"def earlyI : Nat → Assertion\n  | 0 => aFalse\n  | _ + 1 => aTrue\n\ndef neverTrue : BExpr := .equals (.const 0) (.const 1)\n\ntheorem earlyI_step (n : Nat) :\n    Hoare (aAnd (earlyI (n + 1)) (bTrue neverTrue)) .skip (earlyI n) := by\n  intro σ h hpre\n  have hb : BExpr.eval σ neverTrue = true := hpre.2\n  cases hb\n\ntheorem earlyI_stop : ∀ σ h, earlyI 0 σ h → neverTrue.eval σ = false := by\n  intro σ h hI\n  exact hI.elim\n\ntheorem no_I0_rule :\n    ¬ (∀ n, Hoare (earlyI n) (.loop neverTrue .skip) (aAnd (earlyI 0) (bFalse neverTrue))) := by\n  intro hall\n  obtain ⟨s', _, hfalse, _⟩ := hall 1 (fun _ => 0) Heap.empty trivial\n  exact hfalse\n\ntheorem earlyI_ok :\n    ∀ n, Hoare (earlyI n) (.loop neverTrue .skip)\n      (aExists fun m => aAnd (earlyI m) (bFalse neverTrue)) :=\n  hoare_while_variant earlyI_step earlyI_stop"},

    {t:'note', kind:'key', title:'What the index does and does not promise',
     h:"The index is an upper bound on the number of turns, not a count of them. <code>I n</code> holding at entry means the loop runs <i>at most</i> <code>n</code> more times; it may run fewer, and the postcondition's <code>∃ m</code> is what lets the index it reports be larger than zero. Termination comes from the bound, and only from the bound: nothing in the rule says the guard has to survive until the index reaches zero."},

    {t:'p', h:"That is the whole statement. The proof is one induction over ten tactic lines, three of which close a branch by handing over a derivation of the loop's run that the same line assembles."},

    /* ======================================================== m13-4 ==== */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'m13-4',
     name:'hoare_while_variant',
     hard:true,
     why:"The last rule of the program logic, and the only one whose proof <i>constructs</i> a run of unbounded length. Every total triple you have proved so far named its final state outright — one <code>Exec.assign</code>, one <code>Exec.write</code>, a fixed number of <code>Exec.seq</code>s. Here the derivation has a height the statement cannot fix, and the induction is what supplies the missing storeys: <code>Exec.loopTrue</code> in the step case, <code>Exec.loopFalse</code> at the bottom. Both of the remaining exercises on this page are one application of what you prove here.",
     setup:"Two premises, named <code>hstep</code> and <code>hstop</code>, and the conclusion is quantified over the starting index. Nothing here inverts anything: there is no run in the hypotheses to take apart, so every branch has to end by handing over three things it produced itself — a final state, a derivation, and an index.",
     goal:"theorem hoare_while_variant {I : Nat → Assertion} {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd}\n    (hstep : ∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (I (n + 1)) (bTrue b)) c (I n))\n    (hstop : ∀ σ h, I 0 σ h → b.eval σ = false) :\n    ∀ n, Hoare (I n) (.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, the conclusion says: for every index <code>n</code>, every store and heap satisfying <code>I n</code>, there is a state <code>s'</code> such that the loop runs from <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to <code>s'</code> and some index <code>m</code> has <code>I m</code> true at <code>s'</code> together with the guard false there. Three things are yours to produce in every branch: the final state, the derivation, and the index.",
       "Induct on the index. At zero, the stop obligation says the guard is already false, so the loop runs no turns at all and the final state is the state you started in. At <code>n + 1</code> the guard decides: if it is false the same thing happens, and the index you report is <code>n + 1</code>, not zero. If it is true, the step obligation gives a run of the body ending somewhere satisfying <code>I n</code>, and the induction hypothesis — which is the whole theorem at index <code>n</code> — gives a run of the loop from there. Glue the two together.",
       "<code>induction n with | zero | succ n ih</code>, and inside the <code>succ</code> branch <code>cases hbv : b.eval σ with | true | false</code>. The two loop constructors are <code>Exec.loopFalse</code>, which takes a proof that the guard is false, and <code>Exec.loopTrue</code>, which takes that proof for <code>true</code>, a run of the body, and a run of the loop. The saved equation <code>hbv</code> is spent twice in the <code>true</code> branch — once as the second component of the step obligation's precondition, once as <code>Exec.loopTrue</code>'s first argument.",
       "<code>intro n</code>, then <code>induction n with</code>. The <code>zero</code> branch is <code>intro σ h hI</code> followed by a single five-component bracket: <code>exact ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.loopFalse (hstop σ h hI), 0, hI, hstop σ h hI⟩</code> — the state, the derivation, the index, and the two halves of the postcondition, with the stop obligation used twice because it proves both the derivation's premise and the postcondition's second half."
     ],
     sol:"theorem hoare_while_variant {I : Nat → Assertion} {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd}\n    (hstep : ∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (I (n + 1)) (bTrue b)) c (I n))\n    (hstop : ∀ σ h, I 0 σ h → b.eval σ = false) :\n    ∀ n, Hoare (I n) (.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) := by\n  intro n\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n      intro σ h hI\n      exact ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.loopFalse (hstop σ h hI), 0, hI, hstop σ h hI⟩\n  | succ n ih =>\n      intro σ h hI\n      cases hbv : b.eval σ with\n      | true =>\n          obtain ⟨s₁, hex₁, hI₁⟩ := hstep n σ h ⟨hI, hbv⟩\n          obtain ⟨s₂, hex₂, hpost⟩ := ih s₁.store s₁.heap hI₁\n          exact ⟨s₂, Exec.loopTrue hbv hex₁ hex₂, hpost⟩\n      | false =>\n          exact ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.loopFalse hbv, n + 1, hI, hbv⟩",
     solNote:"All three branch endings are a single bracket. The work is in knowing what goes in the slots, and the slot that catches people is the third — the index you report, which is <code>0</code> in the base case, <code>n + 1</code> when the guard goes false early, and, in the remaining branch, whatever the induction hypothesis chose.",
     expl:"The induction is on the index, and the index is not mentioned by the command, so nothing here needs Unit 26's manoeuvre: <code>Nat</code> is an ordinary inductive type and <code>n</code> is an ordinary variable. What the induction produces is a derivation. In the <code>zero</code> branch and in the guard-false branch it is a one-storey derivation, <code>Exec.loopFalse</code>. In the guard-true branch it is <code>Exec.loopTrue</code> over a run of the body — supplied by the step obligation — and a run of the loop — supplied by the induction hypothesis, which is the same theorem one index lower. The tower is exactly as tall as the starting index allows, and the postcondition's existential is what lets a shorter one be reported.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro n', h:"Fixes the starting index. The conclusion is now a single triple, and <code>n</code> is what the induction will run on."},
       {tac:'induction n with', h:"Two branches, on an ordinary <code>Nat</code>. The hypothesis <code>ih</code> in the second branch is the whole triple at index <code>n</code> — a statement about the loop, not about the body."},
       {tac:'| zero =>', h:"The index says no turns remain."},
       {tac:'intro σ h hI', h:"The three binders of a total triple: store, heap, precondition. The goal becomes the existential over final states."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.loopFalse (hstop σ h hI), 0, hI, hstop σ h hI⟩', h:"Five components flattened into one bracket. The final state is the initial one, because the loop runs no turns. <code>Exec.loopFalse</code> needs the guard to be false, which is exactly what the stop obligation gives at index zero. Then the index <code>0</code>, then <code>I 0</code> at the final state — which is <code>hI</code>, since the state did not move — and then the guard being false again. The stop obligation appears twice because the same fact is both a premise of the derivation and half of the postcondition."},
       {tac:'| succ n ih =>', h:"At most <code>n + 1</code> turns remain, and <code>ih</code> settles what happens after one of them."},
       {tac:'intro σ h hI', h:"Same three binders. What is different from the <code>zero</code> branch is that <code>hstop</code> says nothing here: it is a fact about index zero and the index is <code>n + 1</code>."},
       {tac:'cases hbv : b.eval σ with', h:"The guard is a <code>Bool</code>, so two branches — and the equation has to be saved, because both branches need to <i>know</i> what the guard evaluated to. A bare <code>cases b.eval σ</code> would split the goal and discover nothing."},
       {tac:'| true =>', h:"The loop takes a turn."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨s₁, hex₁, hI₁⟩ := hstep n σ h ⟨hI, hbv⟩', h:"Applied the step obligation. Its precondition is <code>aAnd (I (n+1)) (bTrue b)</code> — a pair, and the pair is <code>hI</code> together with the saved equation, because <code>bTrue b σ h</code> is by definition <code>b.eval σ = true</code>. Out comes a state <code>s₁</code>, a run of the body ending there, and <code>I n</code> holding there. This is the line that consumes the totality of the premise: <code>hex₁</code> exists because the premise promised a run."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨s₂, hex₂, hpost⟩ := ih s₁.store s₁.heap hI₁', h:"Applied the induction hypothesis at the state the body left. It returns a run of the <i>whole loop</i> from there and the postcondition at its end. <code>s₁</code> is fed in as its two projections; the state Lean then builds is <code>⟨s₁.store, s₁.heap⟩</code>, which matches <code>s₁</code> by structure eta, and that is why <code>Exec.loopTrue</code> accepts <code>hex₁</code> and <code>hex₂</code> side by side."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨s₂, Exec.loopTrue hbv hex₁ hex₂, hpost⟩', h:"The final state is where the recursive run ended. The derivation is one new storey on top of it: guard true, body ran, loop ran again. The postcondition travels through untouched — the index it reports was chosen inside <code>ih</code>, not here."},
       {tac:'| false =>', h:"The guard is off at index <code>n + 1</code>: the loop stops early."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.loopFalse hbv, n + 1, hI, hbv⟩', h:"The same five-slot shape as the <code>zero</code> branch, with one difference that is the whole point of the existential postcondition: the index reported is <code>n + 1</code>. There is no proof of <code>I 0</code> available here and none is needed."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'hoare_while_variant, six steps (the five shared context lines are dropped from every state)',
        start:"I : Nat → Assertion\nb : BExpr\nc : Cmd\nhstep : ∀ (n : Nat), Hoare (aAnd (I (n + 1)) (bTrue b)) c (I n)\nhstop : ∀ (σ : Store) (h : Heap), I 0 σ h → BExpr.eval σ b = false\n⊢ ∀ (n : Nat), Hoare (I n) (Cmd.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b))",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro n',
           state:"n : Nat\n⊢ Hoare (I n) (Cmd.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b))",
           h:"One triple, with the index a variable of the goal. This is what makes the induction ordinary."},
          {tac:'induction n with',
           state:"case zero\n⊢ Hoare (I 0) (Cmd.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b))",
           h:"Two goals; only the first is shown, as it is throughout this trace. The base case is not a special case of the loop: it is the claim that a loop started at index zero terminates, and the stop obligation is what makes that true."},
          {tac:'intro σ h hI',
           state:"case zero\nσ : Store\nh : Heap\nhI : I 0 σ h\n⊢ ∃ s', Exec (Cmd.loop b c) { store := σ, heap := h } s' ∧ aExists (fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) s'.store s'.heap",
           h:"The existential the whole page is about. Three things have to be produced, and every branch produces all three at once in a single bracket."},
          {tac:'| succ n ih =>',
           state:"case succ\nn : Nat\nih : Hoare (I n) (Cmd.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b))\n⊢ Hoare (I (n + 1)) (Cmd.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b))",
           h:"<code>ih</code> is a triple about the loop, not about the body — which is why the proof needs both premises and the hypothesis: the premise runs the body once, the hypothesis runs the loop for the rest."},
          {tac:'obtain ⟨s₂, hex₂, hpost⟩ := ih s₁.store s₁.heap hI₁',
           state:"case succ.true\ns₁ : State\nhex₁ : Exec c { store := σ, heap := h } s₁\nhI₁ : I n s₁.store s₁.heap\ns₂ : State\nhex₂ : Exec (Cmd.loop b c) { store := s₁.store, heap := s₁.heap } s₂\nhpost : aExists (fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) s₂.store s₂.heap\n⊢ ∃ s', Exec (Cmd.loop b c) { store := σ, heap := h } s' ∧ aExists (fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) s'.store s'.heap",
           h:"Three tactics on from the previous step — the guard split, then both <code>obtain</code>s — and only the six hypotheses they introduce are shown; <code>σ</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>hI</code>, <code>hbv</code>, <code>n</code> and <code>ih</code> sit above them, unchanged. Two runs are now in the context and the goal wants one. <code>hex₁</code> ends where <code>hex₂</code> begins, so <code>Exec.loopTrue</code> composes them."},
          {tac:'| false =>',
           state:"case succ.false\nn : Nat\nih : Hoare (I n) (Cmd.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b))\nσ : Store\nh : Heap\nhI : I (n + 1) σ h\nhbv : BExpr.eval σ b = false\n⊢ ∃ s', Exec (Cmd.loop b c) { store := σ, heap := h } s' ∧ aExists (fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)) s'.store s'.heap",
           h:"Identical to the <code>zero</code> branch's goal except that the only invariant in scope is at index <code>n + 1</code>. <code>ih</code> is still here and is not used: the loop stops now, so there is no later turn for it to settle. That mismatch between <code>I (n + 1)</code> and the <code>I 0</code> a tidier postcondition would demand is what the existential absorbs, and it is where the <code>I 0</code> version dies."}
        ],
        done:"No goals."},
       {t:'detail', title:'What goes wrong if the body only has to preserve the index', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
         {t:'p', h:"The premise asks the body to take <code>I (n + 1)</code> to <code>I n</code>. Ask instead that it take <code>I n</code> to <code>I n</code> — the invariant rule's premise, with the family carried along unchanged — keep the stop obligation as it is, and the resulting statement is false. The witness is the family from the early-exit section above, run against a guard that is true at every store and a body that does nothing."},
         {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Unit 36&rsquo;s premise inside Unit 37&rsquo;s conclusion, refuted. The first theorem is Unit 36&rsquo;s <code>loop_invariant</code> spent on a loop that has no run at all; the second feeds that loop to the weakened rule at index 1, where both premises hold and the conclusion promises a run.',
          src:"def alwaysTrue : BExpr := .equals (.const 0) (.const 0)\n\ntheorem noRun_alwaysTrue {s s' : State} : ¬ Exec (.loop alwaysTrue .skip) s s' := by\n  intro hex\n  have hbody : PartialHoare (aAnd aTrue (bTrue alwaysTrue)) .skip aTrue :=\n    fun σ h s'' _ _ => trivial\n  have hstuck := (loop_invariant hbody hex rfl trivial).2\n  have hone : BExpr.eval s'.store alwaysTrue = true := rfl\n  rw [hone] at hstuck\n  cases hstuck\n\ntheorem preserving_index_is_false :\n    ¬ (∀ (I : Nat → Assertion) (b : BExpr) (c : Cmd),\n        (∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (I n) (bTrue b)) c (I n)) →\n        (∀ σ h, I 0 σ h → b.eval σ = false) →\n        ∀ n, Hoare (I n) (.loop b c) (aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b))) := by\n  intro hall\n  have hstep : ∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (earlyI n) (bTrue alwaysTrue)) .skip (earlyI n) := by\n    intro n σ h hpre\n    cases n with\n    | zero => exact hpre.1.elim\n    | succ m => exact ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.skip, trivial⟩\n  have hstop : ∀ σ h, earlyI 0 σ h → alwaysTrue.eval σ = false := by\n    intro σ h hI\n    exact hI.elim\n  obtain ⟨s', hex, _⟩ :=\n    hall earlyI alwaysTrue .skip hstep hstop 1 (fun _ => 0) Heap.empty trivial\n  exact noRun_alwaysTrue hex"},
         {t:'p', h:"Read the two premises at the weakened rule. The step obligation is vacuous at index 0, because nothing satisfies <code>earlyI 0</code>, and trivial at every higher index, because <code>skip</code> runs and <code>aTrue</code> holds after it. The stop obligation is vacuous for the same reason as the first. So both hold, and the conclusion at index 1 claims a run of a loop whose guard never goes false. The <code>n + 1</code> in the real premise is what stops the family from being carried up a tower with no top."}
       ]}
     ],
     pitfall:"Reporting the index as <code>0</code> in the guard-false branch of <code>succ</code>. It reads as the natural thing to write — the loop has stopped, so surely no turns remain — but the fourth slot then needs a proof of <code>I 0</code> at a state where all you have is <code>I (n + 1)</code>, and the error lands on <code>hI</code>, two slots later than the mistake: <code>Application type mismatch: The argument hI has type I (n + 1) σ h but is expected to have type I 0 { store := σ, heap := h }.store { store := σ, heap := h }.heap</code>. Nothing in that message mentions the <code>0</code> you typed, so the reflex is to go looking at the invariant. Read the expected type instead: it is the invariant at the index you asked for. · The other one is <code>cases b.eval σ</code> without the <code>hbv :</code>. It splits the goal into two and neither branch learns anything — the context after the split is character for character the context before it, with no hypothesis about the guard anywhere in it, because nothing in the goal mentions <code>b.eval σ</code> to be rewritten. Only the saved-equation form leaves a fact behind, and this proof needs that fact twice.",
     variants:"Weaken <code>hstep</code> to <code>PartialHoare</code> and the guard-true branch has nothing to build a run of the body from. The first <code>obtain</code> fails before <code>Exec.loopTrue</code> is ever reached — that is the mismatch printed earlier on this page, where the precondition pair is read as a <code>State</code>. Skip that line and go straight to the constructor, <code>refine ⟨⟨σ, h⟩, Exec.loopTrue hbv ?_ ?_, ?_⟩</code>, and Lean stops on the constructor itself with <code>don't know how to synthesize implicit argument `s'`</code> and a context whose goal is the single word <code>State</code>: it is asking which state the body reached, and a partial premise never names one. · Replace <code>I (n + 1)</code> by <code>I n</code> in the step obligation's precondition — so the body <i>preserves</i> the index rather than dropping it — and the rule becomes false, with a witness compiled in the fold below. · Drop <code>hstop</code> and it is false again, against the same never-stopping loop and with an even simpler family: take <code>I n</code> to be <code>aTrue</code> at every index. The step obligation holds because <code>skip</code> runs and <code>aTrue</code> survives it, and the conclusion at index 0 then claims a run of a loop that has none. <code>hstop</code> is the only premise that ever forces the family to meet a state where the guard is off."
    },

    /* ================================================== worked example ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The worked example: a loop that stops for a reason'},

    {t:'p', h:"Unit 36 left <code>countdown</code> with a partial specification and a warning attached: replace the decrement by an increment and the same four lines prove the same triple, for a loop that never stops. The variant rule is what tells the two apart, and the place it tells them apart is the step obligation. Here is the program again."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:'Unit 36&rsquo;s loop. The guard is <i>not</i> zero; the body decrements. Nothing about it changes on this page.',
     src:"def counterGuard (x : Var) : BExpr := .not (.equals (.var x) (.const 0))\n\ndef countdown (x : Var) : Cmd :=\n  .loop (counterGuard x) (.assign x (.minus (.var x) (.const 1)))"},

    {t:'p', h:"The invariant writes itself, which is why this is the worked example rather than an exercise in invention: the index is the value of the counter. <code>I n</code> is <i>the store holds <code>n</code> at <code>x</code></i> — a claim about the store only, with no memory in it at all, so <code>fun n σ _ => σ x = n</code> and no <code>fact</code> wrapper is needed."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:'The specification. Read the postcondition as: the counter holds some value, and the guard is false at it — which, once the guard is unfolded, says the counter is zero.',
     src:"theorem countdown_spec (x : Var) : ∀ n,\n    Hoare (fun σ _ => σ x = n) (countdown x)\n      (aExists fun m => aAnd (fun σ _ => σ x = m) (bFalse (counterGuard x))) := by"},

    {t:'p', h:"The proof is one application of the rule and then its two obligations, each ending in a single <code>simp</code>. It is worked in full below, because the two obligations are the two shapes every use of this rule produces, and the second is the one place in this course where a <code>BExpr</code> guard has to be opened by hand."},

    {t:'trace', title:'countdown_spec, eight steps. Contexts are trimmed to the lines that changed and the trimmed ones are unchanged throughout.',
     start:"x : Var\n⊢ ∀ (n : Val),\n    Hoare (fun σ x_1 => σ x = n) (countdown x) (aExists fun m => aAnd (fun σ x_1 => σ x = m) (bFalse (counterGuard x)))",
     steps:[
       {tac:'refine hoare_while_variant (I := fun n σ _ => σ x = n) ?_ ?_',
        state:"case refine_1\nx : Var\n⊢ ∀ (n : Nat),\n    Hoare (aAnd (fun σ x_1 => σ x = n + 1) (bTrue (counterGuard x))) (Cmd.assign x ((Atom.var x).minus (Atom.const 1)))\n      fun σ x_1 => σ x = n",
        h:"The loop is gone and what is left is a triple about the body alone — one command, one turn. Both <code>I</code> and <code>b</code> are in fact determined by the postcondition, so <code>(I := …)</code> could be omitted and the same two goals appear; it is written because the family is the one thing on this page a reader has to choose, and a proof that hides its choice teaches nothing. What a reader plausibly tries instead is <code>apply</code>, which this course does not have, or <code>exact hoare_while_variant ?_ ?_</code>, which will not elaborate the holes."},
       {tac:'intro n σ h hpre',
        state:"case refine_1\nn : Nat\nσ : Store\nh : Heap\nhpre : aAnd (fun σ x_1 => σ x = n + 1) (bTrue (counterGuard x)) σ h\n⊢ ∃ s',\n    Exec (Cmd.assign x ((Atom.var x).minus (Atom.const 1))) { store := σ, heap := h } s' ∧\n      (fun σ x_1 => σ x = n) s'.store s'.heap",
        h:"The three binders of a total triple, plus the index. The precondition arrives as one name; it is a pair. Introducing it with the pattern <code>⟨hx, _⟩</code> in this line would work equally and the corpus splits it on the next, so the two halves can be named where they are discussed."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hx, _⟩ := hpre',
        state:"case refine_1\nhx : σ x = n + 1\nright✝ : bTrue (counterGuard x) σ h",
        h:"The invariant half is kept and the guard half is discarded, unnamed — and that is a fact about this program rather than about the rule: the guard is what makes a decrement sound in general, and here it is not needed: at index <code>n + 1</code> the counter is <code>n + 1</code>, which is already positive. Only the two new context lines are shown; the goal is unchanged."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨⟨Store.set σ x (σ x - 1), h⟩, Exec.assign, ?_⟩',
        state:"case refine_1\n⊢ (fun σ x_1 => σ x = n) { store := σ.set x (σ x - 1), heap := h }.store { store := σ.set x (σ x - 1), heap := h }.heap",
        h:"Named the final state, gave the derivation, left the postcondition as the hole. Writing the state out is what keeps this goal to one line: <code>refine ⟨_, Exec.assign, ?_⟩</code> compiles too, and leaves the same goal printed through <code>Atom.eval</code> and two nested record literals, eleven lines instead of one."},
       {tac:'show Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n',
        state:"case refine_1\n⊢ σ.set x (σ x - 1) x = n",
        h:"Stripped the two projections off the state literal and the lambda off the assertion. The goal is now arithmetic. The <code>show</code> is not required — <code>simp [Store.set, hx]</code> closes the previous goal directly — and it is here because the line after it is the one that spends the invariant, and a reader should be able to see what it is spending it on."},
       {tac:'have hx\' : σ x = n + 1 := hx',
        state:"case refine_1\nhx\' : σ x = n + 1\n⊢ σ.set x (σ x - 1) x = n",
        h:"A definitional restatement, and here it costs nothing and buys nothing: <code>hx</code>'s type is the lambda <code>fun σ _ => σ x = n + 1</code> applied to two arguments, <code>simp</code> beta-reduces it on sight, and <code>simp [Store.set, hx]</code> closes the goal with the line deleted. Where the same line stops being optional is a proof whose store fact arrives wrapped in a plain <code>def</code>: <code>simp</code> will not open one, so the hypothesis is reported unused and <code>⊢ σ x - 1 = n</code> is left standing. The capstone below is such a proof."},
       {tac:'simp [Store.set, hx\']',
        state:"No goals.",
        h:"<code>Store.set</code> is a conditional whose condition is <code>x = x</code>, so it collapses; rewriting with <code>hx'</code> turns the goal into <code>n + 1 - 1 = n</code>, and Unit 18's truncated subtraction settles it — the one place in the course where <code>n + 1 - 1 = n</code> has to be true by definition rather than by a lemma."},
       {tac:'intro σ h hI; have hx : σ x = 0 := hI',
        state:"case refine_2\nx : Var\nσ : Store\nh : Heap\nhI : σ x = 0\nhx : σ x = 0\n⊢ BExpr.eval σ (counterGuard x) = false",
        h:"The second obligation, opened under its own bullet. The restatement is not load-bearing here — <code>hI</code> already displays as the equation — but it is the shape the third exercise on this page needs, where <code>hI</code> is not itself the equation and something has to be got out of it first. What a reader tries next is <code>simp [Atom.eval, hx]</code>, and it reports <code>simp made no progress</code>: there is no <code>Atom.eval</code> anywhere in the display and no occurrence of <code>σ x</code> to rewrite, because both are hidden inside <code>counterGuard</code>."}
     ],
     done:"No goals."},

    {t:'p', h:"So the guard has to be opened by hand, and opening it is three unfoldings at once. <code>counterGuard x</code> is <code>.not (.equals (.var x) (.const 0))</code>, and <code>BExpr.eval</code> takes <code>.equals a b</code> to <code>a.eval σ == b.eval σ</code> — where <code>==</code> is the boolean equality test, which for <code>Nat</code> is <code>decide</code> applied to the proposition. So the goal underneath the fold is a boolean negation of a decision procedure, and that is what the <code>show</code> has to say."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:'The last <code>show</code> in the course. Two definitions and one decision procedure opened in a single line, and the equality it claims is definitional — nothing is being proved here, only displayed.',
     src:"    show (!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.var x) = Atom.eval σ (.const 0)))) = false\n    simp [Atom.eval, hx]"},

    {t:'state', cap:'What the <code>show</code> leaves. Context lines unchanged and dropped. <code>simp [Atom.eval, hx]</code> now has something to work on: the two <code>Atom.eval</code>s reduce to <code>σ x</code> and <code>0</code>, <code>hx</code> rewrites the first to <code>0</code>, and <code>0 = 0</code> decides to <code>true</code>, whose negation is <code>false</code>.',
     src:"⊢ (!decide (Atom.eval σ (Atom.var x) = Atom.eval σ (Atom.const 0))) = false"},

    {t:'p', h:"There is a shorter route that never writes the <code>show</code>: <code>simp [counterGuard, BExpr.eval, Atom.eval, hx]</code> names the two definitions in the bracket and closes the goal in one line. It compiles, and it is the right thing to write when you already believe the goal. The <code>show</code> is preferred here because a reader who does not believe it needs to see what <code>bFalse (counterGuard x)</code> is a claim about, and the bracket version never prints it."},

    {t:'p', h:"The <i>other</i> obligation's closing line, <code>simp [Store.set, hx']</code>, is where Unit 18's two lines on truncated subtraction are spent. At an index of the form <code>n + 1</code> the decrement is exact; at zero it does nothing at all, so a body that ran at zero would sit there for ever. The step obligation never has to rule that out — its precondition is <code>I (n + 1)</code>, so the counter is positive before the guard is consulted, which is why the proof above discards the guard conjunct. What the guard is for is the <i>other</i> obligation: it is the only reason <code>I 0 ⟹ ¬b</code> is provable at all."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The two faces of <code>n - 1</code> on <code>Nat</code>. The first is what the step obligation needs; the second is what the guard exists to prevent.',
     src:"example (σ : Store) (x : Var) (n : Nat) (hx : σ x = n + 1) :\n    Atom.eval σ ((Atom.var x).minus (Atom.const 1)) = n := by\n  simp [Atom.eval, hx]\n\nexample (σ : Store) (x : Var) (hx : σ x = 0) :\n    Atom.eval σ ((Atom.var x).minus (Atom.const 1)) = 0 := by\n  simp [Atom.eval, hx]"},

    {t:'p', h:"The finished theorem says <code>countdown</code> stops, which is what Unit 36's partial triple about the same loop could not say. The specification is quantified over the starting index, and the index can be instantiated with the counter's own value at any store — which means <i>every</i> store terminates, with no hypothesis at all."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'One term. Instantiating the index at <code>σ x</code> makes the precondition <code>σ x = σ x</code>, discharged by <code>rfl</code>, and what comes back is a run of the loop from an arbitrary state.',
     src:"example (x : Var) (σ : Store) (h : Heap) :\n    ∃ s', Exec (countdown x) ⟨σ, h⟩ s' ∧\n      (aExists fun m => aAnd (fun σ (_ : Heap) => σ x = m) (bFalse (counterGuard x)))\n        s'.store s'.heap :=\n  countdown_spec x (σ x) σ h rfl"},

    {t:'p', h:"And the increment loop, which Unit 36 showed satisfies the same partial triple, fails the step obligation — not for want of a proof, but because the obligation is false."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The step obligation for a body that increments. At index 1 the invariant says the counter is 1; after the body it is 2, and the obligation demands 0.',
     src:"theorem countUp_step_false (x : Var) :\n    ¬ (∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (fun σ (_ : Heap) => σ x = n + 1) (bTrue (counterGuard x)))\n              (.assign x (.plus (.var x) (.const 1))) (fun σ (_ : Heap) => σ x = n)) := by\n  intro hall\n  obtain ⟨s', hex, hpost⟩ := hall 0 (fun _ => 1) Heap.empty ⟨rfl, rfl⟩\n  cases hex\n  have hf : Store.set (fun _ => 1) x 2 x = 0 := hpost\n  simp [Store.set] at hf"},

    /* ======================================================== m13-5 ==== */

    {t:'p', h:"Now type it. Each obligation ends in a single <code>simp</code> and everything above this line is available; what the exercise measures is whether the shape of the two goals is in your hands rather than on the page."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'m13-5',
     name:'countdown_spec',
     why:"The template for every use of the rule, and the smallest one there is. Both obligations are about a single assignment, the invariant is one equation, and no memory appears anywhere — so the only thing in the way is the machinery of applying the rule and opening a guard. The capstone below runs the same rule against a body with one more command in it, and everything that is hard there is hard because of the family you have to choose, not because of this machinery. Get the machinery free here.",
     setup:"The invariant is <code>fun n σ _ => σ x = n</code> — the counter's value <i>is</i> the number of turns left. Supply it with a named argument to <code>hoare_while_variant</code> and leave both obligations as holes. The first hole is a triple about the assignment; the second is a claim about the guard at index zero, and the <code>simp</code> you would reach for first reports that it made no progress.",
     goal:"theorem countdown_spec (x : Var) : ∀ n,\n    Hoare (fun σ _ => σ x = n) (countdown x)\n      (aExists fun m => aAnd (fun σ _ => σ x = m) (bFalse (counterGuard x))) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, the statement says: for every <code>n</code>, every store holding <code>n</code> at <code>x</code>, and every heap, there is a state the loop runs to at which the counter holds some value <code>m</code> and the guard evaluates to <code>false</code>. The heap is never mentioned in either the precondition or the postcondition and never changes.",
       "Apply the variant rule with the family <i>the counter holds <code>n</code></i>. That leaves two things to prove. One: entered with the counter at <code>n + 1</code> and the guard true, one run of the body leaves the counter at <code>n</code>. Two: if the counter is zero then the guard is false. The first is arithmetic on the store; the second is arithmetic inside a boolean.",
       "<code>refine hoare_while_variant (I := fun n σ _ => σ x = n) ?_ ?_</code>, and then in each hole <code>intro</code> the binders. The body is an assignment, so the final state can be named outright and <code>Exec.assign</code> is the derivation. The second hole will not respond to <code>simp</code> until a <code>show</code> has restated it: what is folded up in <code>BExpr.eval σ (counterGuard x)</code> is a boolean negation of a <code>decide</code>.",
       "The first hole opens <code>intro n σ h hpre</code> then <code>obtain ⟨hx, _⟩ := hpre</code>, and its final state is <code>⟨Store.set σ x (σ x - 1), h⟩</code>, with <code>Exec.assign</code> as the derivation and the postcondition left as a hole. <code>simp [Store.set, hx]</code> closes that hole. The second is the one that needs the <code>show</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem countdown_spec (x : Var) : ∀ n,\n    Hoare (fun σ _ => σ x = n) (countdown x)\n      (aExists fun m => aAnd (fun σ _ => σ x = m) (bFalse (counterGuard x))) := by\n  refine hoare_while_variant (I := fun n σ _ => σ x = n) ?_ ?_\n  · intro n σ h hpre\n    obtain ⟨hx, _⟩ := hpre\n    refine ⟨⟨Store.set σ x (σ x - 1), h⟩, Exec.assign, ?_⟩\n    show Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n\n    have hx' : σ x = n + 1 := hx\n    simp [Store.set, hx']\n  · intro σ h hI\n    have hx : σ x = 0 := hI\n    show (!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.var x) = Atom.eval σ (.const 0)))) = false\n    simp [Atom.eval, hx]",
     solNote:"Four of these thirteen lines can go and the proof still compiles: <code>(I := …)</code>, because the postcondition determines the family; <code>have hx'</code>, because <code>simp</code> beta-reduces the hypothesis it restates; and either <code>show</code>, provided the definitions it opens go into the <code>simp</code> bracket instead. They are written because the shortest proof of a theorem is not always the one that says what the theorem is about — and because the capstone below needs three of the four for real.",
     expl:"One application and two obligations. The first obligation is a triple about a single assignment, so its final state is written out and <code>Exec.assign</code> is the whole derivation; what remains is that the store, updated at <code>x</code> with <code>σ x - 1</code>, holds <code>n</code> at <code>x</code> — true because <code>σ x</code> is <code>n + 1</code>. The second obligation is not about a command at all: it is the claim that the guard is off when the counter is zero, and its entire difficulty is that both the guard and the boolean equality test are folded up behind names that <code>simp</code> will not open unprompted.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'refine hoare_while_variant (I := fun n σ _ => σ x = n) ?_ ?_', h:"Matched the goal against the rule's conclusion, which fixes <code>b</code> to <code>counterGuard x</code> and <code>c</code> to the assignment by unfolding <code>countdown x</code>, and left the two premises as holes. The family is supplied explicitly even though the postcondition determines it."},
       {tac:'· intro n σ h hpre', h:"The first hole. Four binders: the index, then the three of a total triple."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hx, _⟩ := hpre', h:"Split the precondition. The invariant half is <code>hx</code>; the guard half is discarded, because a counter at <code>n + 1</code> is already positive and the proof never has to ask whether the guard fired."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨⟨Store.set σ x (σ x - 1), h⟩, Exec.assign, ?_⟩', h:"Named the final state — store updated at <code>x</code>, heap untouched — gave the one derivation an assignment has, and left the postcondition as the hole. Naming the state rather than writing <code>_</code> is what keeps that hole one line long."},
       {tac:'show Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n', h:"Restated the hole as the arithmetic underneath it, stripping the assertion's lambda and the two projections out of the state literal."},
       {tac:'have hx\' : σ x = n + 1 := hx', h:"Restated the invariant as the equation it definitionally is, and nothing here needs it — see the worked example above. It is written because it names the fact the next line spends, and because the capstone's invariant hides the same fact behind a plain <code>def</code>, where the restatement stops being optional."},
       {tac:'simp [Store.set, hx\']', h:"<code>Store.set</code>'s conditional collapses at <code>x = x</code>, <code>hx'</code> replaces <code>σ x</code> by <code>n + 1</code>, and <code>n + 1 - 1 = n</code> holds on <code>Nat</code>."},
       {tac:'· intro σ h hI', h:"The second hole. No index and no command: three binders, of which the heap is never used."},
       {tac:'have hx : σ x = 0 := hI', h:"The same restatement as above, on the family at index zero."},
       {tac:'show (!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.var x) = Atom.eval σ (.const 0)))) = false', h:"Opened the guard and the boolean test in one line. <code>counterGuard x</code> is a negation of an equality test, <code>BExpr.eval</code> sends <code>.equals</code> to <code>==</code>, and <code>==</code> on <code>Nat</code> is <code>decide</code> of the proposition. All three unfoldings are definitional, so the <code>show</code> is accepted without a proof."},
       {tac:'simp [Atom.eval, hx]', h:"Now there is something to work on: the two <code>Atom.eval</code>s reduce to <code>σ x</code> and to <code>0</code>, <code>hx</code> turns the first into <code>0</code>, the decision on <code>0 = 0</code> is <code>true</code>, and its negation is <code>false</code>."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'The second obligation, three steps (context lines unchanged and dropped after the first)',
        start:"case refine_2\nx : Var\n⊢ ∀ (σ : Store) (h : Heap), σ x = 0 → BExpr.eval σ (counterGuard x) = false",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro σ h hI',
           state:"case refine_2\nx : Var\nσ : Store\nh : Heap\nhI : σ x = 0\n⊢ BExpr.eval σ (counterGuard x) = false",
           h:"Nothing about a run appears: the stop obligation is a fact about stores. The heap binder is introduced and never used, which is what makes this obligation transfer unchanged to the capstone below, where the invariant does own memory."},
          {tac:'have hx : σ x = 0 := hI',
           state:"case refine_2\nhI : σ x = 0\nhx : σ x = 0\n⊢ BExpr.eval σ (counterGuard x) = false",
           h:"Two hypotheses of the same displayed type, and a goal that has not moved. Here the restatement is redundant; in the capstone's stop obligation the same line has to reach inside <code>hI</code> first, and there it is not."},
          {tac:'show (!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.var x) = Atom.eval σ (.const 0)))) = false',
           state:"⊢ (!decide (Atom.eval σ (Atom.var x) = Atom.eval σ (Atom.const 0))) = false",
           h:"Three definitions opened at once. Before this line <code>simp [Atom.eval, hx]</code> reports <code>`simp` made no progress</code>, because neither <code>Atom.eval</code> nor <code>σ x</code> occurs in the display — both are sealed inside <code>counterGuard</code>, and <code>simp</code> was given neither name."}
        ],
        done:"No goals."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Reaching for the second <code>show</code> and writing the guard out from memory. <code>counterGuard x</code> is <code>.not (.equals (.var x) (.const 0))</code>, so the negation is on the outside and the constant is on the <i>right</i> of the equality; write <code>(!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.const 0) = Atom.eval σ (.var x)))) = false</code> and Lean answers <code>'show' tactic failed, pattern … is not definitionally equal to target BExpr.eval σ (counterGuard x) = false</code>, because <code>0 = σ x</code> and <code>σ x = 0</code> are different propositions and no amount of unfolding turns one into the other. The way out is not to guess: put <code>counterGuard</code> and <code>BExpr.eval</code> in the <code>simp</code> bracket, read what is left, and then write the <code>show</code> that matches it.",
     variants:"Drop the second <code>show</code> and the proof stops with <code>`simp` made no progress</code>; widen the bracket to <code>simp [counterGuard, BExpr.eval, Atom.eval, hx]</code> and it closes with no <code>show</code> at all. · Drop the first <code>show</code> and nothing happens, because the state was named and the goal underneath it is already one line: <code>simp [Store.set, hx']</code> closes it either way. Write <code>_</code> for the state instead and that changes. The <code>refine</code> still succeeds, but its hole now prints in eleven lines — the same record literal twice, once projected to <code>.store</code> and once to <code>.heap</code> — and the closing <code>simp</code> no longer finishes: it reports <code>hx'</code> unused and leaves <code>⊢ Atom.eval σ ((Atom.var x).minus (Atom.const 1)) = n</code>. Naming the state is what evaluates the update to <code>σ x - 1</code>; with <code>_</code> the update is still an unreduced <code>Atom.eval</code>, so there is no <code>σ x</code> for <code>hx'</code> to rewrite. · Drop <code>(I := …)</code> and everything still works: the postcondition <code>aExists fun m => aAnd (fun σ _ => σ x = m) (bFalse (counterGuard x))</code> matches <code>aExists fun m => aAnd (I m) (bFalse b)</code> and fixes both. Do not conclude from the corpus proof that Lean needs telling. · Change the guard to <i>the counter is not one</i> and the stop obligation becomes false, since a counter at zero passes that guard: the loop would run from zero, decrement to zero, and spin."
    },

    /* ======================================================== capstone ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The capstone: a loop that owns a cell'},

    {t:'p', h:"Every invariant on this page so far has been a claim about the store alone. This loop will not take one. Its body decrements the counter and then writes into the cell at <code>l</code>, and <code>Exec.write</code>'s premise is that <code>l</code> is allocated — so a family that never mentions the heap admits states at which the body has no run at all, and the step obligation is a total triple, which means it has to produce one."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:'The program. Two commands in the body; the loop is Unit 36&rsquo;s guard over it. The address is a literal, as every address in this language is.',
     src:"def drainBody (x : Var) (l : Loc) : Cmd :=\n  .assign x (.minus (.var x) (.const 1)) ;; .write l (.var x)\n\ndef drain (x : Var) (l : Loc) : Cmd := .loop (counterGuard x) (drainBody x l)"},

    {t:'p', h:"You are given the program and the rule. What you have to invent is the family, and then the two obligations follow mechanically. Three rungs: state the invariant; prove the step obligation; prove the stop obligation. The specification is then one application, and it is a term rather than a tactic proof."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x73',
     name:'drain_spec, with drain_step and drain_stop as staged rungs',
     hard:true,
     why:"The terminal capstone. Everything the course has built meets here: an inductively defined semantics, a triple, the loop rule, an assertion that owns memory, and a program whose termination is proved rather than assumed. It is also the only exercise in the course whose invariant carries a store fact and an ownership claim at once — every earlier invariant was one or the other — and inventing it is the part that cannot be mechanised, because the rule takes the family as a parameter and offers no way to compute one.",
     setup:"<b>A design exercise: write the invariant yourself, then the theorems.</b> Four declarations, in this order. First <code>def drainInv (x : Var) (l : Loc) (n : Nat) : Assertion</code>, the family. Then <code>theorem drain_step</code>, the step obligation for <code>drainBody x l</code> at every index. Then <code>theorem drain_stop</code>, the guard being false at index zero. Then <code>theorem drain_spec</code>, which is one application of <code>hoare_while_variant</code> and needs no tactics. The editor starts you with a placeholder; delete it and write all four. Your own text is what gets checked, so a different correct invariant passes too.",
     goal:"-- Delete the line below and write your own `def drainInv …`, then\n-- `theorem drain_step …`, `theorem drain_stop …` and `theorem drain_spec …`.\n-- Any invariant from which the two obligations follow will be accepted.\nexample : True := by",
     hints:[
       "There is no goal to restate, so restate the problem. The loop is <code>drain x l</code>; its body decrements <code>x</code> and then writes the new value of <code>x</code> into the cell at <code>l</code>. You need a family <code>drainInv x l : Nat → Assertion</code> such that (i) entered at index <code>n + 1</code> with the guard true, one run of the body ends at a state satisfying the family at index <code>n</code>, and (ii) at index zero the guard is false. Both obligations are about the body only; the loop never appears again after the rule is applied.",
       "The index has to be the counter, because that is the only quantity that goes down. So one conjunct is <i>the store holds <code>n</code> at <code>x</code></i>. But the body writes to <code>l</code>, and a write only runs where the address is allocated, so the family must also claim the cell — and it must say what the cell holds, because the postcondition of a turn has to pin it down for the next turn to start from. After one turn the counter is <code>n</code> and the cell has been given the counter's value, so the cell holds <code>n</code> too. The two conjuncts describe the same heap, so they are joined by ordinary conjunction rather than by a separating one.",
       "The invariant is <code>def drainInv (x : Var) (l : Loc) (n : Nat) : Assertion := aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ x = n)) (l ↦ n)</code>. The step obligation opens by destructuring the precondition into the pair-of-a-pair it is, <code>⟨⟨hx, hh⟩, _⟩</code>; the second component is an equation between heaps, so <code>subst</code> it. The derivation is <code>Exec.seq</code> of an <code>Exec.assign</code> and an <code>Exec.write</code>, and the write needs a lookup: <code>singleton_same l (n + 1)</code>, at the value the cell held <i>before</i> the turn. Then two holes, one per conjunct, each opened with a <code>show</code>, and the heap one closes with Unit 06's <code>write_singleton</code>.",
       "<code>theorem drain_step (x : Var) (l : Loc) (n : Nat) : Hoare (aAnd (drainInv x l (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard x))) (drainBody x l) (drainInv x l n)</code>, proved by <code>intro σ h hpre</code>, <code>obtain ⟨⟨hx, hh⟩, _⟩ := hpre</code>, <code>have hx' : σ x = n + 1 := hx</code>, <code>subst hh</code>, and then <code>refine ⟨_, Exec.seq Exec.assign (Exec.write (singleton_same l (n + 1))), ?_, ?_⟩</code>. That leaves the two conjuncts of the invariant at the final state, both printed through five nested record literals, and both repaired by a <code>show</code>. The other two statements are shorter than their names suggest: <code>theorem drain_stop (x : Var) (l : Loc) : ∀ σ h, drainInv x l 0 σ h → (counterGuard x).eval σ = false</code>, which is <code>countdown_spec</code>'s second obligation with <code>hI.1</code> in place of <code>hI</code>; and <code>drain_spec</code>, whose statement is the rule's conclusion with <code>drainInv x l</code> for <code>I</code> and <code>drain x l</code> for the loop, and whose proof is <code>hoare_while_variant</code> applied to the two theorems above — no <code>by</code>, no tactics."
     ],
     sol:"def drainInv (x : Var) (l : Loc) (n : Nat) : Assertion :=\n  aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ x = n)) (l ↦ n)\n\ntheorem drain_step (x : Var) (l : Loc) (n : Nat) :\n    Hoare (aAnd (drainInv x l (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard x)))\n          (drainBody x l) (drainInv x l n) := by\n  intro σ h hpre\n  obtain ⟨⟨hx, hh⟩, _⟩ := hpre\n  have hx' : σ x = n + 1 := hx\n  subst hh\n  refine ⟨_, Exec.seq Exec.assign (Exec.write (singleton_same l (n + 1))), ?_, ?_⟩\n  · show Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n\n    simp [Store.set, hx']\n  · show Heap.write (Heap.singleton l (n + 1)) l (Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x)\n        = Heap.singleton l n\n    have hset : Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n := by simp [Store.set, hx']\n    rw [hset, write_singleton]\n\ntheorem drain_stop (x : Var) (l : Loc) :\n    ∀ σ h, drainInv x l 0 σ h → (counterGuard x).eval σ = false := by\n  intro σ h hI\n  have hx : σ x = 0 := hI.1\n  show (!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.var x) = Atom.eval σ (.const 0)))) = false\n  simp [Atom.eval, hx]\n\ntheorem drain_spec (x : Var) (l : Loc) : ∀ n,\n    Hoare (drainInv x l n) (drain x l)\n      (aExists fun m => aAnd (drainInv x l m) (bFalse (counterGuard x))) :=\n  hoare_while_variant (fun n => drain_step x l n) (drain_stop x l)",
     solNote:"If your invariant differed and Lean accepted your two obligations, you were right — the editor runs your text, and there is more than one family that works. Compare it with this one anyway on one point: whether the number indexing the store fact is the same number indexing the cell. It has to be, because the body copies one into the other.",
     expl:"Three declarations and an application. <code>drain_step</code> is the only one with any work in it, and the work is bookkeeping of the kind Unit 29 turned into a discipline: destructure, substitute the heap equation so the goal names a concrete singleton, build the two-command derivation, and repair the two goal displays with <code>show</code> before touching them. The heap conjunct closes on <code>write_singleton</code> — writing to the one cell a singleton heap has gives another singleton — once the value being written has been computed to <code>n</code>. <code>drain_stop</code> is <code>countdown_spec</code>'s second obligation with <code>hI.1</code> in place of <code>hI</code>. <code>drain_spec</code> names the two and stops.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'def drainInv … := aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ x = n)) (l ↦ n)', h:"The family. <code>fact</code> rather than <code>pure</code>, because the store half must not claim the heap is empty — the other half owns a cell. <code>aAnd</code> rather than <code>∗</code>, because both halves describe the same heap."},
       {tac:'intro σ h hpre', h:"The three binders of a total triple, for the step obligation. The index <code>n</code> is already a parameter of the theorem, so it is not introduced here."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨⟨hx, hh⟩, _⟩ := hpre', h:"The precondition is <code>aAnd (aAnd (fact …) (l ↦ n+1)) (bTrue …)</code>, so a pair whose first component is a pair. <code>hx</code> is the store fact, <code>hh</code> is the ownership claim, and the guard is discarded — as in <code>countdown_spec</code>, the invariant at index <code>n + 1</code> already implies the counter is positive."},
       {tac:'have hx\' : σ x = n + 1 := hx', h:"Restated the store fact as the equation it definitionally is, so that <code>simp</code> can rewrite with it later. Without this line both <code>simp</code>s below report the hypothesis unused."},
       {tac:'subst hh', h:"<code>hh</code> is <code>(l ↦ n + 1) σ h</code>, which unfolds to <code>h = Heap.singleton l (n + 1)</code>, so <code>subst</code> eliminates <code>h</code> everywhere. From here on the heap in the goal is a concrete singleton, which is what makes the write's premise and the closing rewrite both available by name."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨_, Exec.seq Exec.assign (Exec.write (singleton_same l (n + 1))), ?_, ?_⟩', h:"The final state is left as an underscore because the derivation determines it. The derivation is the two commands in order: an assignment, which needs nothing, and a write, which needs a proof that <code>l</code> is allocated in the heap the write sees — that heap is <code>Heap.singleton l (n + 1)</code>, and <code>singleton_same</code> is exactly that lookup. Giving the wrong value here (<code>singleton_same l n</code>) is an application type mismatch, not a later failure."},
       {tac:'· show Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n', h:"First conjunct. The display before this line is the store fact applied to a state built out of five nested record literals; the <code>show</code> replaces it with the lookup that is definitionally the same thing."},
       {tac:'simp [Store.set, hx\']', h:"Same three moves as in <code>countdown_spec</code>: the conditional collapses at <code>x = x</code>, <code>hx'</code> rewrites, and truncated subtraction settles <code>n + 1 - 1 = n</code>."},
       {tac:'· show Heap.write (Heap.singleton l (n + 1)) l (Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x) = Heap.singleton l n', h:"Second conjunct, and the same repair. <code>l ↦ v</code> is an equation between heaps, so what is being claimed is that the heap after the write <i>is</i> the singleton holding <code>n</code>. The value written is still displayed as a store lookup, because nothing has computed it yet."},
       {tac:'have hset : Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n := by simp [Store.set, hx\']', h:"Computed that value, as its own fact, so that it can be rewritten with. This is the first conjunct's proof, promoted to a named equation — the same three moves, spent twice."},
       {tac:'rw [hset, write_singleton]', h:"The first rewrite turns the written value into <code>n</code>, leaving <code>(Heap.singleton l (n + 1)).write l n = Heap.singleton l n</code>. The second is Unit 06's law: writing to the address a singleton holds gives the singleton at the new value. The trailing <code>rfl</code> that <code>rw</code> always tries closes it."},
       {tac:'intro σ h hI', h:"<code>drain_stop</code>. Three binders again, and the heap is never used — the stop obligation is about the store."},
       {tac:'have hx : σ x = 0 := hI.1', h:"The invariant at index zero is a pair; its first component is the store fact. This is where <code>countdown_spec</code>'s apparently redundant restatement earns itself: here the projection is needed and the restatement carries it."},
       {tac:'show (!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.var x) = Atom.eval σ (.const 0)))) = false', h:"Character for character the line from <code>countdown_spec</code>, because the guard is the same guard. Three definitions opened at once: <code>counterGuard</code>, <code>BExpr.eval</code>, and the boolean equality test."},
       {tac:'simp [Atom.eval, hx]', h:"Closes it, as before."},
       {tac:'drain_spec := hoare_while_variant (fun n => drain_step x l n) (drain_stop x l)', h:"No tactic block. The rule wants a function from indices to step obligations and a stop obligation, and that is what the two theorems are. <code>drain_step x l</code> would do as well as the eta-expanded form; the corpus writes the lambda."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'detail', title:'Two invariants that fail, and where', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
         {t:'p', h:"Keep only the store fact — <i>the counter holds <code>n</code></i>, and nothing about memory — and the step obligation fails before any arithmetic, at the run. The body writes to <code>l</code>, <code>Exec.write</code>&rsquo;s premise is that <code>l</code> is allocated, and an invariant that never mentions the heap is satisfied by the empty heap."},
         {t:'p', h:"Own the cell but pin its contents — <i>the counter holds <code>n</code>, and <code>l</code> holds 0</i> — and the run goes through and the heap does not. The body writes the counter&rsquo;s new value, so the cell ends up holding <code>n</code>, and the invariant asked for 0. The two failures are what force the cell&rsquo;s contents to be indexed by the same number as the store fact."},
         {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Both wrong invariants, refuted. The first witness is the empty heap, where the write cannot run; the second is a cell holding 0 with the counter at 2, where the write puts 1 into it. The second uses concrete numerals for <code>x</code> and <code>l</code> so that <code>Store.set</code>&rsquo;s conditional reduces.',
          src:"def storeOnlyInv (x : Var) (n : Nat) : Assertion := fact (fun σ => σ x = n)\n\ntheorem storeOnlyInv_step_false (x : Var) (l : Loc) :\n    ¬ (∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (storeOnlyInv x (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard x)))\n              (drainBody x l) (storeOnlyInv x n)) := by\n  intro hall\n  obtain ⟨s', hex, _⟩ := hall 0 (fun _ => 1) Heap.empty ⟨rfl, rfl⟩\n  cases hex with\n  | seq h₁ h₂ =>\n      cases h₁\n      cases h₂ with\n      | write hl => exact absurd hl (by simp [Heap.empty])\n\ndef flatInv (x : Var) (l : Loc) (n : Nat) : Assertion :=\n  aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ x = n)) (l ↦ 0)\n\ntheorem flatInv_step_false :\n    ¬ (∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (flatInv 0 3 (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard 0)))\n              (drainBody 0 3) (flatInv 0 3 n)) := by\n  intro hall\n  obtain ⟨s', hex, _, hpts⟩ := hall 1 (fun _ => 2) (Heap.singleton 3 0) ⟨⟨rfl, rfl⟩, rfl⟩\n  cases hex with\n  | seq h₁ h₂ =>\n      cases h₁\n      cases h₂\n      have hcell := congrFun hpts 3\n      simp [Heap.write, Heap.singleton, Store.set, Atom.eval] at hcell"},
         {t:'p', h:"Which conjunction joins the two halves is the third decision, and it is <code>aAnd</code>, not <code>∗</code>. A store fact owns no memory, so under a star it would be free to claim an arbitrary heap of its own — Unit 23&rsquo;s argument for <code>pure</code> over <code>fact</code>, from the other direction — and the two halves here are meant to describe the same heap, which is what <code>aAnd</code> says."}
       ]},
       {t:'trace', title:'drain_step, five steps (context lines are shown only where they change)',
        start:"x : Var\nl : Loc\nn : Nat\n⊢ Hoare (aAnd (drainInv x l (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard x))) (drainBody x l) (drainInv x l n)",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro σ h hpre',
           state:"σ : Store\nh : Heap\nhpre : aAnd (drainInv x l (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard x)) σ h\n⊢ ∃ s', Exec (drainBody x l) { store := σ, heap := h } s' ∧ drainInv x l n s'.store s'.heap",
           h:"The existential over final states, with the two-command body inside it. Both the precondition and the postcondition are still folded behind <code>drainInv</code>."},
          {tac:'obtain ⟨⟨hx, hh⟩, _⟩ := hpre',
           state:"right✝ : bTrue (counterGuard x) σ h\nhx : fact (fun σ => σ x = n + 1) σ h\nhh : (l ↦ n + 1) σ h",
           h:"Three names for a two-level pair, the guard among them and discarded. <code>hh</code> is an equation between heaps in disguise, which is what the next move exploits."},
          {tac:'have hx\' : σ x = n + 1 := hx; subst hh',
           state:"σ : Store\nhx' : σ x = n + 1\nright✝ : bTrue (counterGuard x) σ (Heap.singleton l (n + 1))\nhx : fact (fun σ => σ x = n + 1) σ (Heap.singleton l (n + 1))\n⊢ ∃ s', Exec (drainBody x l) { store := σ, heap := Heap.singleton l (n + 1) } s' ∧ drainInv x l n s'.store s'.heap",
           h:"<code>h</code> is gone from the context and every occurrence of it is now a concrete singleton — including inside <code>hx</code>, which mentions the heap and does not care: <code>fact</code> discards its heap argument, so <code>hx'</code> has the same type whichever order the two lines are written in. Both orders compile."},
          {tac:'show Store.set σ x (σ x - 1) x = n',
           state:"case refine_1\n⊢ σ.set x (σ x - 1) x = n",
           h:"The <code>refine</code> has run and the first bullet is open. Without this <code>show</code> the goal prints in forty-nine lines: the store fact applied to the store and to the heap of a record literal that itself contains a record literal containing the initial state, with the whole thing written out twice. Nothing was proved by the <code>show</code>; the two goals are the same goal."},
          {tac:'rw [hset]',
           state:"case refine_2\nhset : σ.set x (σ x - 1) x = n\n⊢ (Heap.singleton l (n + 1)).write l n = Heap.singleton l n",
           h:"The second bullet, after its own <code>show</code> and the <code>have</code> that computes the written value. What is left is one of Unit 06's eleven equations, applied at the only address in play, and <code>rw [write_singleton]</code> closes it."}
        ],
        done:"No goals."},
       {t:'detail', title:'What happens if the body jumps the counter to zero instead of decrementing it', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
         {t:'p', h:"Change the body to <code>x := 0 ; [l] := x</code>. The loop still stops — sooner, in fact — but the step obligation with the natural invariant is <i>false</i>, because the rule insists the index falls by exactly one and this body drops the counter from anything to zero in a single turn. Instantiate the obligation at <code>n = 1</code>: the precondition is the invariant at index 2, so the counter is 2, and after the body it is 0 where the postcondition demands 1."},
         {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The natural invariant, refuted for the jumping body. The witness is a store holding 2 and the index 1.',
          src:"def drainBodyZero (x : Var) (l : Loc) : Cmd :=\n  .assign x (.const 0) ;; .write l (.var x)\n\ntheorem drainZero_step_false (x : Var) (l : Loc) :\n    ¬ (∀ n, Hoare (aAnd (drainInv x l (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard x)))\n              (drainBodyZero x l) (drainInv x l n)) := by\n  intro hall\n  obtain ⟨s', hex, hfact, _⟩ := hall 1 (fun _ => 2) (Heap.singleton l 2) ⟨⟨rfl, rfl⟩, rfl⟩\n  cases hex with\n  | seq h₁ h₂ =>\n      cases h₁\n      cases h₂\n      have hf : Store.set (fun _ => 2) x 0 x = 1 := hfact\n      simp [Store.set] at hf"},
         {t:'p', h:"The repair is not a stronger rule. It is a different index. The index was never obliged to be the counter's value — it is an upper bound on the number of turns left, and this loop takes at most one turn from anywhere. So index the family by <i>turns remaining</i> rather than by the counter: at index zero the counter is zero and the cell holds zero; at every positive index all you know, and all you need, is that the cell is allocated."},
         {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The same rule, a different family, and the jumping loop is verified. Every index above zero is the same assertion, which is legitimate because the index is a bound and not a measurement.',
          src:"def drainZero (x : Var) (l : Loc) : Cmd := .loop (counterGuard x) (drainBodyZero x l)\n\ndef drainZeroInv (x : Var) (l : Loc) : Nat → Assertion\n  | 0 => aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ x = 0)) (l ↦ 0)\n  | _ + 1 => aExists fun v => (l ↦ v)\n\ntheorem drainZero_step (x : Var) (l : Loc) : ∀ n,\n    Hoare (aAnd (drainZeroInv x l (n + 1)) (bTrue (counterGuard x)))\n          (drainBodyZero x l) (drainZeroInv x l n) := by\n  intro n σ h hpre\n  obtain ⟨⟨v, hh⟩, _⟩ := hpre\n  subst hh\n  refine ⟨_, Exec.seq Exec.assign (Exec.write (singleton_same l v)), ?_⟩\n  cases n with\n  | zero =>\n      refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩\n      · show Store.set σ x 0 x = 0\n        simp [Store.set]\n      · show Heap.write (Heap.singleton l v) l (Store.set σ x 0 x) = Heap.singleton l 0\n        have hset : Store.set σ x 0 x = 0 := by simp [Store.set]\n        rw [hset, write_singleton]\n  | succ m =>\n      refine ⟨0, ?_⟩\n      show Heap.write (Heap.singleton l v) l (Store.set σ x 0 x) = Heap.singleton l 0\n      have hset : Store.set σ x 0 x = 0 := by simp [Store.set]\n      rw [hset, write_singleton]\n\ntheorem drainZero_stop (x : Var) (l : Loc) :\n    ∀ σ h, drainZeroInv x l 0 σ h → (counterGuard x).eval σ = false := by\n  intro σ h hI\n  have hx : σ x = 0 := hI.1\n  show (!(decide (Atom.eval σ (.var x) = Atom.eval σ (.const 0)))) = false\n  simp [Atom.eval, hx]\n\ntheorem drainZero_spec (x : Var) (l : Loc) : ∀ n,\n    Hoare (drainZeroInv x l n) (drainZero x l)\n      (aExists fun m => aAnd (drainZeroInv x l m) (bFalse (counterGuard x))) :=\n  hoare_while_variant (drainZero_step x l) (drainZero_stop x l)"},
         {t:'p', h:"Which is the honest account of what the exactly-one restriction costs: not expressive power, but the obligation to invent an index rather than read one off the program. A rule with a well-founded measure would have done that reindexing for you, at the price of the strong induction this course does not have."}
       ]}
     ],
     pitfall:"Giving <code>Exec.write</code> the lookup at the wrong value. <code>singleton_same l n</code> is the cell read at what it will hold <i>after</i> the turn; the write's premise is about the heap the write sees, which still holds <code>n + 1</code>. Lean reports it on the <code>refine</code> line rather than in either hole, and the expected type is printed as a seven-line record projection, so the message is long; the two lines that matter are <code>has type</code> and <code>Heap.singleton l n l = some n</code>, which is a lookup in the wrong heap. A second error follows it, <code>unsolved goals</code> against the original existential, because the <code>refine</code> failed and left the goal where it was; that one is a consequence, not a separate problem, and reading it first costs you the real message. · The second one is the same trap <code>countdown_spec</code> sets, doubled: leave out <code>hx'</code> and both <code>simp</code>s report <code>This simp argument is unused: hx</code> and leave <code>⊢ σ x - 1 = n</code> standing. The store fact arrives wrapped in <code>fact</code>, and <code>simp</code> rewrites with things it can see as equations.",
     variants:"Replace the body's <code>x := x - 1 ; [l] := x</code> by <code>x := 0 ; [l] := x</code> and this invariant's step obligation becomes false — compiled in the fold above — because the index must fall by exactly one and the counter falls further. Re-indexing repairs it, also compiled, and that is the general answer whenever the program's own arithmetic does not decrease in steps of one. · Drop the <code>l ↦ n</code> conjunct, or keep it and pin the value to <code>l ↦ 0</code> at every index, and the step obligation fails either way — at the <i>run</i> in the first case, at the <i>heap equation</i> in the second. Both are compiled in the first fold above. · Reverse the two commands in the body — write first, then decrement — and the cell ends up holding <code>n + 1</code> where the invariant at index <code>n</code> wants <code>n</code>. It fails at the heap conjunct only; the store conjunct survives, because the decrement still happens and its result is what the store fact talks about, whichever order the two commands ran in. The repair is to index the cell one higher than the counter, <code>aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ x = n)) (l ↦ (n + 1))</code>, and then the proof goes through in the same shape, with <code>Exec.seq</code>'s two arguments swapped and the write's lookup taken at <code>n + 2</code>. Its second bullet is a line shorter than this one's: the value written is <code>σ x</code> rather than the store after the decrement, so <code>hx'</code> rewrites it directly and the intermediate <code>have hset</code> is not needed. Same loop, same rule, different family — which is the third time on this page that inventing the index is the whole of the work."
    },

    /* =================================================== retrospective ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Retrospective'},

    {t:'p', h:"Three questions. Answer each before opening it."},

    {t:'detail', title:'(i) Why must the invariant be indexed by the variant, rather than sitting beside it?', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"Because the induction has to be on something, and the only induction on <code>Nat</code> this course has gives the hypothesis at the predecessor. An invariant sitting beside a decreasing expression gives you a fact about the body's arithmetic and a separate fact about the body's effect, and joining them means arguing that a strictly decreasing sequence of naturals terminates — well-founded induction, in which the hypothesis is available at every smaller number."},
      {t:'p', h:"Indexing folds the two into one. The premise <i>entered at <code>n + 1</code>, the body leaves you at <code>n</code></i> is a single triple, and the induction that consumes it is <code>induction n with | zero | succ n ih</code>. The constant family shows the same thing from the other side: with no index there is no descent, and the rule degenerates into a statement about a loop with no turns in it."}
    ]},

    {t:'detail', title:'(ii) What happens to the step obligation if the body is changed to x := 0?', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"With the natural invariant — the counter indexed by its own value — the obligation becomes false, and there is a two-line witness for it: at index 1 the invariant says the counter is 2, and after the body it is 0 where the obligation demands 1. The rule requires the index to fall by exactly one and this body drops it further."},
      {t:'p', h:"The loop still terminates, and it is still provable with this rule. What has to change is the family: index it by the number of turns remaining rather than by the counter's value, which for this loop is at most one from anywhere. Both the refutation and the repaired proof are compiled in the fold inside the exercise above. The general statement is that the index is a <i>bound</i>, and reading it off the program only works when the program decrements by one."}
    ]},

    {t:'detail', title:'(iii) The invariant owns exactly one cell. What would it need to own a linked list?', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"In words: <i>the segment of the list already visited, from the head to the cursor, together with the list still to come, from the cursor onwards</i> — with the index counting the nodes remaining. Unit 33 built both halves and proved they join, so the assertion is available: <code>lseg visited p cur ∗ listRep rest cur</code>, indexed by the length of <code>rest</code>. The step obligation would say that one turn moves one node from the second half to the first, which is Unit 33's maintenance entailment."},
      {t:'p', h:"What stops it is not the assertion language. It is the command language: <code>Cmd.load</code> takes a literal <code>Loc</code>, so no program in this language can read an address out of a variable and follow it, and the loop body that would advance the cursor cannot be written down. Every assertion needed for the proof exists; the program does not. Unit 38 states the one-line change to <code>Cmd</code> that removes the restriction, and says why every theorem in this course survives it."}
    ]},

    {t:'dod', h:"You can say why the number that buys termination goes into the invariant rather than into the program, and what a variant written as an expression would have cost. You can prove <code>hoare_while_variant</code> by induction on the index, and point at the three lines where the loop's derivation is built rather than inverted. You can refute the version whose postcondition is <code>I 0</code> and name the branch that fails. You can apply the rule to a loop, open a <code>BExpr</code> guard with a <code>show</code> that unfolds the boolean decision at the same time, and see where truncated subtraction is spent. You can invent an invariant that carries a store fact and an ownership claim at once, discharge both obligations it generates, and say what fails if either conjunct is dropped or fixed. And you can say what the exactly-one restriction on the index costs, and repair a loop that violates it by re-indexing."},

    {t:'p', h:"The development is complete: a logic, a language, a semantics, the frame rule, verified programs, recursive predicates, the wand, <code>wp</code>, and both loop rules. Two questions are left. Which of it was about heaps? And what would it take to walk that linked list?"}

  ]
});
